{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon's drug decriminalization program, which began as the first in the nation, will end on Sunday. A new law, HB4002, will reinstate criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. Individuals caught with these substances will have the option to be charged with possession or to participate in a treatment program. This program includes completing a behavioral health program and a \"deflection program\" designed to avoid fines.\n\nPossession of small amounts of drugs will be classified as a misdemeanor, potentially leading to up to six months in jail. The law also aims to increase police enforcement in public spaces and impose stricter penalties for drug sales near locations like parks. Counties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment options to redirect individuals from the legal system to addiction and mental health services. This allows local areas to build programs tailored to their available resources.\n\nPolice Chief Bob Day of Portland stated that the criteria for deflection are strict, requiring no other charges, no warrants, no violent behavior, and that the individual is medically stable. If someone qualifies for deflection, officers will contact the county's deflection dispatch line to assess eligibility and connect them with a mobile behavioral health team. This team will have 30 minutes to attempt contact. If this timeframe is not met, jail time will be imposed.\n\nThis change in policy is intended to streamline responses to drug use and potentially reduce the burden on the criminal justice system. However, some argue it could result in inconsistent and unfair application of the law across different areas. The law's effectiveness will be closely watched, as it represents a significant shift in how the country approaches drug policy, and it will impact people of all ages. It's important to consider the implications for how people eat, shop, and communicate, even across a language barrier.\n\nP.P.S.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.001105769770219922}, {"token": "'", "logprob": -0.2590566575527191}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.06318403780460358}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -3.766942609217949e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.024209819734096527}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.33885058760643005}, {"token": "▁which", "logprob": -0.41120198369026184}, {"token": "▁began", "logprob": -0.07791683822870255}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.008472687564790249}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.4841533303260803}, {"token": "▁first", "logprob": -0.2464882880449295}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.0701037272810936}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0005224770284257829}, {"token": "▁nation", "logprob": -0.8211528658866882}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0006140968762338161}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -1.4041707515716553}, {"token": "▁end", "logprob": -0.008046000264585018}, {"token": "▁on", "logprob": -0.23042961955070496}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -1.4066597032069694e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.03924280032515526}, {"token": "▁A", "logprob": -0.0149863101541996}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.0066394442692399025}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.0036912893410772085}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0019116000039502978}, {"token": "▁HB", "logprob": -0.00014256415306590497}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.4543427823809907e-05}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -0.23674830794334412}, {"token": "▁reinstate", "logprob": -0.1104193702340126}, {"token": "▁criminal", "logprob": -0.0024579332675784826}, {"token": "▁penalties"